#import "../../../proofs.typ": speech, footnote

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 1]: Who is the first and chiefest being?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: God is the first and chiefest being.#footnote[Isa 44:6; 48:12; Ps 97:9]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 2]: Ought everyone to believe there is a God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Everyone ought to believe there is a God;#footnote[Heb 11:6] and it is their great sin and folly who do not.#footnote[Ps 14:1]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 3]: How may we know there is a God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The light of nature in man and the works of God plainly declare there is a God;#footnote[Rom 1:19–20; Ps 19:1–3; Acts 17:24] but his word and Spirit only do it fully and effectually for the salvation of sinners.#footnote[1 Cor 2:10; 2 Tim 3:15, 16]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 4]: What is the word of God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The holy scriptures of the Old and New Testament are the word of God, and the only certain rule of faith and obedience.#footnote[2 Tim 3:16; Eph 2:20]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 5]: May all men make use of the holy scriptures?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: All men are not only permitted, but commanded and exhorted to read, hear, and understand the holy scriptures.#footnote[John 5:38; 17:17–18; Rev 1:3; Acts 8:30]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 6]: What things are chiefly contained in the holy scriptures?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The holy scriptures chiefly contain what man ought to believe concerning God, and what duty God requireth of man.#footnote[2 Tim 1:13; 3:15–16]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 7]: What is God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: God is a Spirit,#footnote[John 4:24] infinite,#footnote[Job 11:7, 8, 9] eternal,#footnote[Ps 90:2] and unchangeable#footnote[Jas 1:17] in his being,#footnote[Exod 3:14] wisdom,#footnote[Ps 147:5] power,#footnote[Rev 4:8] holiness,#footnote[Rev 15:4] justice, goodness, and truth.#footnote[Exod 34:6]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 8]: Are there more gods than one?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: There is but one only, the living and true God.#footnote[Deut 6:4; Jer 10:10]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 9]: How many persons are there in the Godhead?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: There are three persons in the godhead, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit; and these three are one God, the same in essence, equal in power and glory.#footnote[1 John 5:7; Matt 28:19]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 10]: What are the decrees of God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The decrees of God are his eternal purpose according to the counsel of his will, whereby, for his own glory, he hath foreordained whatsoever comes to pass.#footnote[Eph 1:4, 11; Rom 9:22–23; Isa 46:10; Lam 3:37]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 11]: How doth God execute his decrees?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: God executeth his decrees in the works of creation and providence.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 12]: What is the work of creation?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The work of creation is God’s making all things of nothing, by the word of his power, in the space of six days, and all very good.#footnote[Gen 1 throughout; Heb 11:3]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 13]: How did God create man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: God created man, male and female, after his own image, in knowledge, righteousness, and holiness, with dominion over the creatures.#footnote[Gen 1:26–28; Col 3:10; Eph 4:24]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 14]: What are God’s works of providence?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: God’s works of providence are his most holy,#footnote[Ps 145:17] wise,#footnote[Isa 28:29; Ps 104:24] and powerful preserving#footnote[Heb 1:3] and governing all his creatures, and all their actions.#footnote[Ps 103:19; Matt 10:29–31]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 15]: What special act of providence did God exercise towards man in the estate wherein he was created?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: When God had created man, he entered into a covenant of life with him upon condition of perfect obedience; forbidding him to eat of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, upon pain of death.#footnote[Gal 3:12; Gen 2:17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 16]: Did our first parents continue in the estate wherein they were created?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Our first parents being left to the freedom of their own will, fell from the estate wherein they were created, by sinning against God.#footnote[Gen 3:6–8, 13; Eccl 7:29]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 17]: What is sin?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Sin is any want of conformity unto, or transgression of, the law of God.#footnote[1 John 3:4]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 18]: What was the sin whereby our first parents fell from the estate wherein they were created?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The sin whereby our first parents fell from the estate wherein they were created, was their eating the forbidden fruit.#footnote[Gen 3:6, 12, 16–17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 19]: Did all mankind fall in Adam’s first transgression?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The covenant being made with Adam, not only for himself but for his posterity, all mankind descending from him by ordinary generation sinned in him, and fell with him in his first transgression.#footnote[Gen 2:16–17; Rom 5:12; 1 Cor 15:21–22]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 20]: Into what estate did the fall bring mankind?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fall brought mankind into an estate of sin and misery.#footnote[Rom 5:12]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 21]: Wherein consists the sinfulness of that estate whereinto man fell?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The sinfulness of that estate whereinto man fell, consists in the guilt of Adam’s first sin, the want of original righteousness, and the corruption of his whole nature, which is commonly called original sin; together with all actual transgressions which proceed from it.#footnote[Rom 5:12; Eph 2:1–3; Jas 1:14–15; Matt 15:19]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 22]: What is the misery of that estate whereinto man fell?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: All mankind by their fall lost communion with God,#footnote[Gen 3:8, 10, 24] are under his wrath and curse,#footnote[Eph 2:2–3; Gal 3:10] and so made liable to all miseries in this life, to death itself, and to the pains of hell for ever.#footnote[Lam 3:39; Rom 6:23; Matt 25:41, 46]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 23]: Did God leave all mankind to perish in the estate of sin and misery?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: God having out of his mere good pleasure, from all eternity, elected some to everlasting life,#footnote[Eph 1:4–5] did enter into a covenant of grace, to deliver them out of the estate of sin and misery, and to bring them into an estate of salvation by a Redeemer.#footnote[Rom 3:20–22; Gal 3:21–22]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 24]: Who is the Redeemer of God’s elect?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The only Redeemer of God’s elect is the Lord Jesus Christ;#footnote[1 Tim 2:5–6] who, being the eternal Son of God, became man,#footnote[John 1:14; Gal 4:4] and so was and continueth to be God and man in two distinct natures, and one person for ever.#footnote[Rom 9:5; Luke 1:35; Col 2:9; Heb 7:24–25]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 25]: How did Christ, being the Son of God become man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ the Son of God became man by taking to himself a true body,#footnote[Heb 2:14, 17; 10:5] and a reasonable soul;#footnote[Matt 26:38] being conceived by the power of the Holy Spirit in the womb of the Virgin Mary, and born of her,#footnote[Luke 1:27, 31, 34–35, 42; Gal 4:4] yet without sin.#footnote[Heb 4:15; 7:26]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 26]: What offices doth Christ execute as our Redeemer?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ as our Redeemer executeth the offices of a prophet, of a priest, and of king, both in his estate of humiliation and exaltation.#footnote[Acts 3:22; Heb 12:25; 2 Cor 13:3; Heb 5:5–7; 7:25; Ps 2:6; Isa 9:6–7; Matt 21:5; Ps 2:8–11]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 27]: How doth Christ execute the office of a prophet?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ executeth the office of prophet in revealing to us, by his word and Spirit, the will of God for our salvation.#footnote[John 1:18; 1 Pet 1:10–12; John 15:15; 20:31]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 28]: How doth Christ execute the office of a priest?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ executeth the office of priest in his once offering up himself a sacrifice to satisfy divine justice#footnote[Heb 9:14, 28] and reconcile us to God,#footnote[Heb 2:17] and in making continual intercession for us.#footnote[Heb 7:24–25]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 29]: How doth Christ execute the office of king?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ executeth the office of a king, in subduing us to himself,#footnote[Acts 15:14–16] in ruling,#footnote[Isa 33:22] and defending us,#footnote[Isa 32:1–2] and in restraining and conquering all his and our enemies.#footnote[1 Cor 15:25; Ps 110 throughout]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 30]: Wherein did Christ’s humiliation consist?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ’s humiliation consisted in his being born, and that in a low condition,#footnote[Luke 2:7] made under the law,#footnote[Gal 4:4] undergoing the miseries of this life,#footnote[Heb 12:2–3; Isa 53:2–3] the wrath of God,#footnote[Luke 22:44; Matt 27:46] and the cursed death of the cross;#footnote[Phil 2:8] in being buried,#footnote[1 Cor 15:3–4] and continuing under the power of death for a time.#footnote[Acts 2:24–27, 31; Matt 12:40]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 31]: Wherein consisteth Christ’s exaltation?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Christ’s exaltation consisteth in his rising again from the dead on the third day,#footnote[1 Cor 15:4] in ascending up into heaven,#footnote[Mark 16:19] in sitting at the right hand of God the Father,#footnote[Eph 1:20] and in coming to judge the world at the last day.#footnote[Acts 1:11, 17:31]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 32]: How are we made partakers of the redemption purchased by Christ?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: We are made partakers of the redemption purchased by Christ, by the effectual application of it to us#footnote[John 1:11–12] by his Holy Spirit.#footnote[Titus 3:5–6]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 33]: How doth the Spirit apply to us the redemption purchased by Christ?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The Spirit applieth to us the redemption purchased by Christ, by working faith in us,#footnote[Eph 1:13–14; John 6:37, 39; Eph 2:8] and thereby uniting us to Christ, in our effectual calling.#footnote[Eph 3:17; 1 Cor 1:9]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 34]: What is effectual calling?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Effectual calling is the work of God’s Spirit,#footnote[2 Tim 1:9; 2 Thess 2:13–14] whereby convincing us of our sin and misery,#footnote[Acts 2:37] enlightening our minds in the knowledge of Christ,#footnote[Acts 26:18] and renewing our wills,#footnote[Ezek 36:26–27] he doth persuade and enable us to embrace Jesus Christ freely offered to us in the gospel.#footnote[John 6:44–45; Phil 2:13]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 35]: What benefits do they that are effectually called partake of in this life?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: They that are effectually called do in this life partake of justification,#footnote[Rom 8:30] adoption,#footnote[Eph 1:5] sanctification, and the several benefits which in this life do either accompany or flow from them.#footnote[1 Cor 1:30]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 36]: What is justification?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Justification is an act of God’s free grace, wherein he pardoneth all our sins,#footnote[Rom 3:24–25; 4:6–8] and accepteth us as righteous in his sight,#footnote[2 Cor 5:19, 21] only for the righteousness of Christ imputed to us,#footnote[Rom 5:17–19] and received by faith alone.#footnote[Gal 2:16; Phil 3:9]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 37]: What is adoption?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Adoption is an act of God’s free grace,#footnote[1 John 3:1] whereby we are received into the number and have a right to all the privileges of the sons of God.#footnote[John 1:12; Rom 8:14–17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 38]: What is sanctification?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Sanctification is the work of God’s free grace,#footnote[2 Thess 2:13] whereby we are renewed in the whole man after the image of God,#footnote[Eph 4:23–24] and are enabled more and more to die unto sin, and live unto righteousness.#footnote[Rom 6:4, 6; 8:1]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 39]: What are the benefits which in this life do accompany or flow from justification, adoption and sanctification?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The benefits which in this life do accompany or flow from justification, adoption, and sanctification, are assurance of God’s love, peace of conscience,#footnote[Rom 5:1–2, 5] joy in the Holy Spirit,#footnote[Rom 5:5, 17] increase of grace,#footnote[Prov 4:18] and perseverance therein to the end.#footnote[1 John 5:13; 1 Pet 1:5]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 40]: What benefits do believers receive from Christ at death?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The souls of believers are at their death made perfect in holiness,#footnote[Heb 12:23] and do immediately pass into glory;#footnote[2 Cor 5:1, 6, 8; Phil 1:23; Luke 23:43] and their bodies being still united to Christ,#footnote[1 Thess 4:14] do rest in their graves#footnote[Isa 57:2] till the resurrection.#footnote[Job 19:26–27]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 41]: What benefits do believers receive from Christ at the resurrection?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: At the resurrection believers, being raised up in glory,#footnote[1 Cor 15:43] shall be openly acknowledged, and acquitted in the day of judgment,#footnote[Matt 25:23; Matt 10:32] and made perfectly blessed, both in soul and body, in the full enjoyment of God#footnote[1 John 3:2; 1 Cor 13:12] to all eternity.#footnote[1 Thess 4:17–18]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 42]: But what shall be done to the wicked at their death?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The souls of the wicked shall, at their death, be cast into the torments of hell, and their bodies lie in their graves, till the resurrection and judgment of the great day.#footnote[Luke 16:23–24; Acts 2:24; Jude 5, 7; 1 Pet 3:19; Ps 49:14]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 43]: What shall be done to the wicked, at the day of judgment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: At the day of judgment the bodies of the wicked, being raised out of their graves, shall be sentenced, together with their souls, to unspeakable torments with the devil and his angels for ever.#footnote[John 5:28–29; Matt 25:41, 46; 2 Thess 1:8–9]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 44]: What is the duty which God requireth of man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The duty which God requireth of man is, obedience to his revealed will.#footnote[Mic 6:8; 1 Sam 15:22]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 45]: What did God at first reveal to man for the rule of his obedience?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The rule which God at first revealed to man for his obedience, was the moral law.#footnote[Rom 2:14–15; 10:5]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 46]: Where is the moral law summarily comprehended?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The moral law is summarily comprehended in the ten commandments.#footnote[Deut 10:4; Matt 19:17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 47]: What is the sum of the ten commandments?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The sum of the ten commandments is, to love the Lord our God, with all our heart, with all our soul, with all our strength, and with all our mind; and our neighbour as ourselves.#footnote[Matt 22:37–40]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 48]: What is the preface to the ten commandments?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The preface to the ten commandments is in these words; I am the Lord thy God which have brought thee out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage.#footnote[Exod 20:2]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 49]: What doth the preface to the ten commandments teach us?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The preface to the ten commandments teacheth us that because God is the Lord, and our God and redeemer, therefore we are bound to keep all his commandments.#footnote[Luke 1:74–75; 1 Pet 1:15–19]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 50]: Which is the first commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The first commandment is, Thou shalt have no other gods before me.#footnote[Exod 20:3]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 51]: What is required in the first commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The first commandment requireth us to know and acknowledge God to be the only true God and our God,#footnote[1 Chr 28:9; Deut 26:17] and to worship and glorify him accordingly.#footnote[Matt 4:10; Ps 29:2]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 52]: What is forbidden in the first commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The first commandment forbiddeth the denying,#footnote[Ps 14:1] or not worshipping and glorifying the true God,#footnote[Rom 1:21] as God and our God,#footnote[Ps 81:10–11] and the giving of that worship and glory to any other, which is due unto him alone.#footnote[Rom 1:25–26]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 53]: What are we especially taught by these words before me, in the first commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: These words before me, in the first commandment teach us, that God, who seeth all things, taketh notice of and is much displeased with the sin of having any other God.#footnote[Exod 8:5ff]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 54]: Which is the second commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The second commandment is, Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, or any likeness of anything that is in heaven above, or that is in the earth beneath, or that is in the water under the earth; thou shalt not bow down thyself to them, nor serve them; for I the Lord thy God am a jealous God, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation of them that hate me; and shewing mercy unto thousands of them that love me, and keep my commandments.#footnote[Exod 20:4–6]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 55]: What is required in the second commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The second commandment requireth the receiving, observing, and keeping pure and entire all such religious worship and ordinances, as God hath appointed in his word.#footnote[Deut 32:46; Matt 23:20; Acts 2:42]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 56]: What is forbidden in the second commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The second commandment forbiddeth the worshipping of God by images#footnote[Deut 4:15–19; Exod 32:5, 8] or any other way not appointed in his word.#footnote[Deut 12:31–32]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 57]: What are the reasons annexed to the second commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The reasons annexed to the second commandment are, God’s sovereignty over us,#footnote[Ps 45:2–3, 6] his propriety in us,#footnote[Ps 45:11] and the zeal he hath to his own worship.#footnote[Exod 34:13–14]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 58]: Which is the third commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The third commandment is, Thou shalt not take the name of the Lord thy God in vain; for the Lord will not hold him guiltless that taketh his name in vain.#footnote[Exod 20:7]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 59]: What is required in the third commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The third commandment requireth the holy and reverent use of God’s names,#footnote[Matt 6:9; Deut 28:58] titles,#footnote[Ps 68:4] attributes,#footnote[Rev 15:3–4] ordinances,#footnote[Mal 1:11, 14] word#footnote[Ps 136:1–2] and works.#footnote[Job 36:24]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 60]: What is forbidden in the third commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The third commandment forbiddeth all profaning and abusing of any thing whereby God makes himself known.#footnote[Mal 1:6–7, 12; 2:2; 3:14]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 61]: What is the reason annexed to the third commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The reason annexed to the third commandment is, that however the breakers of this commandment may escape punishment from men, yet the Lord our God will not suffer them to escape his righteous judgment.#footnote[1 Sam 2:12, 17, 22, 29; 3:13; Deut 28:58–59]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 62]: What is the fourth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fourth commandment is, Remember the Sabbath day to keep it holy; six days shalt thou labour and do all thy work; but the seventh day is the Sabbath of the Lord thy God, in it thou shalt not do any work, thou, nor thy son, nor thy daughter, nor thy man-servant, nor thy maid-servant, nor thy cattle, nor the stranger that is within thy gates; for in six days the Lord made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day; wherefore the Lord blessed the Sabbath day and hallowed it.#footnote[Exod 20:8–11]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 63]: What is required in the fourth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fourth commandment requireth the keeping holy to God one whole day in seven to be a Sabbath to Himself.#footnote[Exod 20:8–11; Deut 5:12–14]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 64]: Which day of the seven hath God appointed to be the weekly Sabbath?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Before the resurrection of Christ, God appointed the seventh day of the week to be the weekly Sabbath;#footnote[Exod 20:8–11; Deut 5:12–14] and the first day of the week ever since, to continue to the end of the world, which is the Christian Sabbath.#footnote[Ps 118:24; Matt 28:1; Mark 2:27–28; John 20:19–20, 26; Rev 1:10; Mark 16:2; Luke 24:1, 30–36; John 20:1; Acts 1:3; 2:1–2; 20:7; 1 Cor 16:1–2]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 65]: How is the Sabbath to be sanctified?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The Sabbath is to be sanctified by a holy resting all that day,#footnote[Exod 20:8, 10] even from such worldly employments and recreations as are lawful on other days;#footnote[Exod 16:25–28; Neh 13:15–22] and spending the whole time in the public and private exercises of God’s worship,#footnote[Luke 4:16; Acts 20:7; Psalm 92:title; Isa 66:23] except so much as is to be taken up in the works of necessity and mercy.#footnote[Matt 12:1–13]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 66]: What is forbidden in the fourth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fourth commandment forbiddeth the omission or careless performance of the duties required,#footnote[Ezek 22:26; Amos 8:5; Mal 1:13] and the profaning the day by idleness,#footnote[Acts 20:7, 9] or doing that which is in itself sinful,#footnote[Ezek 23:38] or by unnecessary thoughts, words, or works, about worldly employments or recreations.#footnote[Jer 17:24–27; Isa 58:13]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 67]: What are the reasons annexed to the fourth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The reasons annexed to the fourth commandment, are God’s allowing us six days of the week for our own lawful employments,#footnote[Exod 20:9] his challenging a special propriety in a seventh, his own example, and his blessing the Sabbath day.#footnote[Exod 20:11]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 68]: Which is the fifth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fifth commandment is, Honor thy father and thy mother; that thy days may be long in the land which the Lord thy God giveth thee.#footnote[Exod 20:12]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 69]: What is required in the fifth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fifth commandment requireth the preserving the honour and performing the duties belonging to every one in their several places and relations, as superiors,#footnote[Eph 5:21] inferiors,#footnote[1 Pet 2:17] or equals.#footnote[Rom 12:10]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 70]: What is forbidden in the fifth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The fifth commandment forbiddeth the neglect of, or doing any thing against the honour and duty which belongeth to every one in their several places and relations.#footnote[Matt 15:4–6; Ezek 34:24; Rom 13:8]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 71]: What is the reason annexed to the fifth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The reason annexed to the fifth commandment is a promise of long life and prosperity (as far as it shall serve for God’s glory, and their own good) to all such as keep this commandment.#footnote[Deut 5:16; Eph 6:2–3]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 72]: What is the sixth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The sixth commandment is, Thou shalt not kill.#footnote[Exod 20:13]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 73]: What is required in the sixth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The sixth commandment requireth all lawful endeavours to preserve our own life#footnote[Eph 5:28–29] and the life of others.#footnote[1 Kgs 18:4]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 74]: What is forbidden in the sixth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The sixth commandment absolutely forbiddeth the taking away of our own life, or the life of our neighbour unjustly, or whatsoever tendeth thereunto.#footnote[Acts 26:28; Gen 9:9]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 75]: Which is the seventh commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The seventh commandment is, Thou shalt not commit adultery.#footnote[Exod 20:14]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 76]: What is required in the seventh commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The seventh commandment requireth the preservation of our own and our neighbour’s chastity, in heart, speech, and behavior.#footnote[1 Cor 7:2–3, 5, 34, 36; Col 4:6; 1 Pet 3:2]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 77]: What is forbidden in the seventh commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The seventh commandment forbiddeth all unchaste thoughts, words, and actions.#footnote[Matt 15:19; 5:28; Eph 5:3–4]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 78]: Which is the eighth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The eighth commandment is, Thou shalt not steal.#footnote[Exod 20:15]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 79]: What is required in the eighth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The eighth commandment requireth the lawful procuring and furthering the wealth and outward estate of ourselves and others.#footnote[Gen 30:30; 1 Tim 5:8; Lev 25:35; Deut 22:1–5; Exod 23:4–5; Gen 47:14, 20]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 80]: What is forbidden in the eighth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The eighth commandment forbiddeth whatsoever doth or may unjustly hinder our own#footnote[1 Tim 5:8; Prov 28:19] or our neighbour’s wealth or outward estate.#footnote[Prov 21:17, 23:20–21; Eph 4:28]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 81]: Which is the ninth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The ninth commandment is, Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour.#footnote[Exod 20:16]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 82]: What is required in the ninth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The ninth commandment requireth the maintaining and promoting of truth between man and man,#footnote[Zech 8:16] and of our own and our neighbour’s good name,#footnote[John 5:12] especially in witness bearing.#footnote[Prov 14:5, 25]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 83]: What is forbidden in the ninth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The ninth commandment forbiddeth whatsoever is prejudicial to the truth, or injurious to our own or our neighbour’s good name.#footnote[1 Sam 17:28; Lev 19:16; Ps 15:2–3]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 84]: Which is the tenth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The tenth commandment is Thou shalt not covet thy neighbour’s house, thou shalt not covet thy neighbour’s wife, nor his man-servant, nor his maid-servant, nor his ox, nor his ass, nor anything that is thy neighbour’s.#footnote[Exod 20:17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 85]: What is required in the tenth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The tenth commandment requireth full contentment with our own condition,#footnote[Heb 13:5; 1 Tim 6:6] with a right and charitable frame of spirit toward our neighbour, and all that is his.#footnote[Job 31:29; Rom 7:15; 1 Tim 1:5; 1 Cor 8:4, 7]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 86]: What is forbidden in the tenth commandment?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The tenth commandment forbiddeth all discontentment with our own estate,#footnote[1 Kgs 21:4; Esth 5:13; 1 Cor 10:10] envying or grieving at the good of our neighbour,#footnote[Gal 5:26; Jas 3:14, 16] and all inordinate motions and affections to anything that is his.#footnote[Rom 7:7–8, 13:9; Deut 5:21]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 87]: Is any man able perfectly to keep the commandments of God?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: No mere man since the fall is able in this life perfectly to keep the commandments of God,#footnote[Eccl 7:20; 1 John 1:8, 10; Gal 5:17] but doth daily break them in thought, word, or deed.#footnote[Gen 6:5, 8:21; Rom 3:9–21; Jas 3:2–13]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 88]: Are all transgressions of the law equally heinous?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Some sins in themselves, and by reason of several aggravations, are more heinous in the sight of God than others.#footnote[Ezek 8:6, 13, 15; 1 John 5:16; Ps 78:17, 32, 56]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 89]: What doth every sin deserve?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Every sin deserveth God’s wrath and curse, both in this life and that which is to come.#footnote[Eph 5:6; Gal 3:10; Lam 3:39; Matt 25:41; Rom 6:23]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 90]: What doth God require of us that we may escape his wrath and curse, due to us for sin?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: To escape the wrath and curse of God due to us for sin, God requireth of us faith in Jesus Christ, repentance unto life,#footnote[Acts 20:21] with the diligent use of all the outward means whereby Christ communicateth to us the benefits of redemption.#footnote[Prov 2:1–6, 8:33 to the end; Isa 55:2–3]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 91]: What is faith in Jesus Christ?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Faith in Jesus Christ is a saving grace,#footnote[Heb 10:39] whereby we receive and rest upon him alone for salvation, as he is offered to us in the gospel.#footnote[John 1:12; Isa 26:3–4; Phil 3:9; Gal 2:16]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 92]: What is repentance unto life?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Repentance unto life is a saving grace,#footnote[Acts 11:28] whereby a sinner, out of a true sense of his sin,#footnote[Acts 2:37–38] and apprehension of the mercy of God in Christ,#footnote[Joel 2:12; Jer 3:22] doth, with grief and hatred of his sin, turn from it unto God,#footnote[Jer 31:18–19; Ezek 36:31] with full purpose of and endeavour after new obedience.#footnote[2 Cor 7:11; Isa 1:16–17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 93]: What are the outward means whereby Christ communicateth to us the benefits of redemption?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The outward and ordinary means whereby Christ communicateth to us the benefits of redemption are his ordinances, especially the word, baptism, the Lord’s supper, and prayer; all which means are made effectual to the elect for salvation.#footnote[Matt 28:19–20; Acts 2:42, 46–47]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 94]: How is the word made effectual to salvation?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The Spirit of God maketh the reading, but especially the preaching of the word, an effectual means of convincing and converting sinners, and of building them up in holiness and comfort through faith unto salvation.#footnote[Neh 8:8; Acts 26:18; Ps 19:8; Acts 20:32; Rom 1:15–16; 10:13–17; 15:4; 1 Cor 14:24–25; 2 Tim 3:15–17]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 95]: How is the word to be read and heard, that it may become effectual to salvation?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: That the word may become effectual to salvation, we must attend thereunto with diligence,#footnote[Prov 8:34] preparation,#footnote[1 Pet 2:1–2] and prayer;#footnote[Ps 119:18] receive it with faith and love,#footnote[Heb 4:2; 2 Thess 2:10] lay it up in our hearts,#footnote[Ps 119:18] and practice it in our lives.#footnote[Luke 8:15; Jas 1:25]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 96]: How do baptism and the Lord’s supper become effectual means of salvation?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Baptism and the Lord’s supper become effectual means of salvation, not for any virtue in them, or in him that doth administer them, but only by the blessing of Christ,#footnote[1 Pet 3:21; Matt 3:11; 1 Cor 3:6–7] and the working of the Spirit in those that by faith receive them.#footnote[1 Cor 12:3; Matt 28:19]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 97]: What is baptism?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Baptism is an ordinance of the New Testament instituted by Jesus Christ, to be unto the party baptized a sign of his fellowship with him, in his death, burial, and resurrection; of his being ingrafted into him;#footnote[Rom 6:3–5; Col 2:12; Gal 3:27] of remission of sins;#footnote[Mark 1:4; Acts 2:38, 22:16] and of his giving up himself unto God through Jesus Christ, to live and walk in newness of life.#footnote[Rom 6:3–4]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 98]: To whom is baptism to be administered?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Baptism is to be administered to all those who actually profess repentance towards God,#footnote[Acts 2:38; Matt 3:6] faith in and obedience to our Lord Jesus Christ, and to none other.#footnote[Acts 8:12, 36–38; 10:47–48]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 99]: Are the infants of such as are professing believers to be baptized?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The infants of such as are professing believers are not to be baptized, because there is neither command or example in the holy scriptures, or certain consequence from them to baptize such.#footnote[Prov 30:6; Luke 3:7–8]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 100]: How is Baptism rightly administered?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Baptism is rightly administered by immersion, or dipping the whole body of the party in water, into the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit, according to Christ’s institution, and the practice of the apostles,#footnote[Matt 3:16; John 3:23; 4:1–2; Matt 28:19–20; Acts 8:38; Rom 6:4; Col 2:12] and not by sprinkling or pouring of water, or dipping some part of the body, after the tradition of men.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 101]: What is the duty of such who are rightly baptized?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: It is the duty of such who are rightly baptized to give up themselves to some particular and orderly church of Jesus Christ, that they may walk in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord blameless.#footnote[Acts 2:41–42; 5:13–14; 9:26; 1 Pet 2:5; Luke 1:6]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 102]: What is the Lord’s supper?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The Lord’s supper is an ordinance of the New Testament, instituted by Jesus Christ; wherein by giving and receiving bread and wine, according to his appointment, his death is shown forth, and the worthy receivers are, not after a corporal and carnal manner, but by faith, made partakers of his body and blood, with all his benefits, to their spiritual nourishment and growth in grace.#footnote[Matt 26:26–28; 1 Cor 11:23–26; 10:16]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 103]: Who are the proper subjects of this ordinance?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: They who have been baptized upon a personal profession of their faith in Jesus Christ, and repentance from dead works.#footnote[Acts 2:41–42]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 104]: What is required to the worthy receiving of the Lord’s supper?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: It is required of them that would worthily partake of the Lord’s supper, that they examine themselves of their knowledge to discern the Lord’s body,#footnote[1 Cor 11:28–29] of their faith to feed upon him,#footnote[2 Cor 13:5] of their repentance,#footnote[1 Cor 11:31] love,#footnote[1 Cor 10:16–17] and new obedience,#footnote[1 Cor 5:7–8] lest coming unworthily they eat and drink judgment to themselves.#footnote[1 Cor 11:28–29]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 105]: What is prayer?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: Prayer is an offering up our desires to God,#footnote[Ps 62:8] by the assistance of the Holy Spirit,#footnote[Rom 8:26] for things agreeable to his will,#footnote[1 John 5:14; Rom 8:27] in the name of Christ,#footnote[John 16:23] believing,#footnote[Matt 21:22; Jas 1:6] with confession of our sins,#footnote[Ps 32:5–6; Dan 9:4] and thankful acknowledgments of his mercies.#footnote[Phil 4:6]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 106]: What rule hath God given for our direction in prayer?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The whole word of God is of use to direct us in prayer;#footnote[1 John 5:14] but the special rule of direction is that prayer which Christ taught his disciples, commonly called the Lord’s prayer.#footnote[Matt 6:9–13; Luke 11:2–4]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 107]: What doth the preface of the Lord’s prayer teach us?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The preface of the Lord’s prayer, which is Our Father which art in heaven,#footnote[Matt 6:9] teacheth us to draw near to God with all holy reverence and confidence, as children to a father, able and ready to help us;#footnote[Rom 8:15; Luke 11:13; Isa 24:8] and that we should pray with and for others.#footnote[Acts 12:5; 1 Tim 2:1–2]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 108]: What do we pray for in the first petition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: In the first petition, which is, Hallowed be thy name,#footnote[Matt 6:9] we pray that God would enable us and others to glorify him in all that whereby he maketh himself known,#footnote[Ps 67:2–3] and that he would dispose all things to his own glory.#footnote[Ps 83 throughout; Rom 11:36]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 109]: What do we pray for in the second petition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: In the second petition, which is, Thy kingdom come,#footnote[Matt 6:10] we pray that Satan’s kingdom may be destroyed,#footnote[Ps 68:1, 18] and that the kingdom of grace may be advanced#footnote[Rev 12:10–11] ourselves and others brought into it and kept in it,#footnote[2 Thess 3:1; Rom 10:1; John 17:19–20] and that the kingdom of glory may be hastened.#footnote[Rev 22:10]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 110]: What do we pray for in the third petition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: In the third petition, which is, Thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven#footnote[Matt 6:10] we pray that God by his grace would make us able and willing to know, obey, and submit to his will in all things,#footnote[Ps 67 throughout; Ps 119:36; 2 Sam 15:25; Job 1:21] as the angels do in heaven.#footnote[Ps 103:20–21]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 111]: What do we pray for in the fourth petition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: In the fourth petition, which is, Give us this day our daily bread,#footnote[Matt 6:11] we pray that of God’s free gift we may receive a competent portion of the good things of this life, and enjoy his blessing with them.#footnote[Prov 30:8; Gen 28:20; 1 Tim 4:4–5]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 112]: What do we pray for in the fifth petition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: In the fifth petition, which is, And forgive us our debts as we forgive our debtors,#footnote[Matt 6:12] we pray that God, for Christ’s sake, would freely pardon all our sins#footnote[Ps 51:1–2, 7, 9; Dan 9:17–19] which we are rather encouraged to ask because of his grace we are enabled from the heart to forgive others.#footnote[Luke 11:4; Matt 18:35]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 113]: What do we pray for in the sixth petition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: In the sixth petition, which is, And lead us not into temptation but deliver us from evil,#footnote[Matt 6:13] we pray that God would either keep us from being tempted to sin,#footnote[Matt 26:31] or support and deliver us when we are tempted.#footnote[2 Cor 12:8]]

#speech[#smallcaps[Question 114]: What doth the conclusion of the Lord’s prayer teach us?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Answer]: The conclusion of the Lord’s prayer, which is, For Thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, forever. Amen,#footnote[Matt 6:13] teacheth us to take our encouragement in prayer from God only,#footnote[Dan 9:4, 7–9, 16–19] and in our prayers to praise Him, ascribing kingdom, power, and glory, to Him.#footnote[1 Chr 29:10–13] And in testimony of our desire and assurance to be heard, we say, Amen.#footnote[1 Cor 4:16; Rev 22:20–21]]
